Restore the cost of termination checks whose measure is one integer or a tuple of integers (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/414). The corrected decreases order (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/264) had emitted these checks as a single assertion that conjoins the lower bound with the decrease, and in some proof contexts the solver took more than 30 times as long on it. For a strict check at a call or at the end of a loop iteration whose components are all int-based, Dafny now emits the shape it emitted before the corrected order: a bounded-below assertion for each component, then the lexicographic decrease. The two shapes are the same formula (docs/dev/decreases-order.md, "Integer tuples"), so what is provable does not change, but the solver's cost does, in both directions: two proofs in the test suite that finished under the single assertion run out of resources under the split one. A negative integer measure is again reported as "decreases expression ... must be bounded below by 0". Real-valued components, `by O` components, nonincrease checks, induction hypotheses and loop summaries keep the corrected encoding.
